{state.status === "retired" ? <StateEvent view={state} /> : <BaseLayout title={`${event.title} | The Catch`} current="Events">
  <div slot="locator" class="locator"><a href="/events/">Series</a><a href="/events/lisa-cook/">Lisa Cook and the Fed</a><span>October 9, 2026</span></div>
  <div class="content">
    <main class="story" id="story-root">
      <p class="story-kicker" data-layer="fact">Lisa Cook and the Fed &middot; October 9, 2026 &middot; updated {event.updated}</p>
      <h1 data-layer="fact">{event.title}</h1>
      <p class="story-dek" data-layer="fact">{event.dek}</p>
      <ReadingModes />
      <nav class="story-toc" aria-label="In this story" data-layer="fact">
        <details class="toc-disclosure" open><summary><span>In this story</span><span class="toc-current" aria-hidden="true"></span></summary>
        <ol>
          <li><a href="#what-happened">What happened</a></li>
          <li><a href="#the-mortgages">Who Cook is, and the charge against her</a></li>
          <li><a href="#why-now">Why a hearing now</a></li>
          <li><a href="#the-sides">Is a mortgage form reason to fire her?</a></li>
          <li><a href="#the-catch">The catch</a></li>
          <li><a href="#next">What happens next</a></li>
          <li><a href="#records">The records</a></li>
        </ol>
        </details>
      </nav>

      <section id="what-happened">
        <h2 data-layer="fact">What happened</h2>
        <p data-layer="narrative" data-answer="1">President Trump has ordered Lisa Cook, a governor of the Federal Reserve, to appear at a closed hearing at the White House on November 5. She will be questioned about allegations that she made false statements on mortgages in 2021. The law lets a president fire a Fed governor only "for cause," and the three officials Trump named to hear her case will recommend whether he has it.<Cite s="wh-memo-2026-10-07" passage="I hereby establish a committee of inquiry (Committee) to investigate allegations that Federal Reserve Governor Lisa D. Cook made false statements in connection with one or more mortgage instruments." /><Cite s="wh-memo-2026-10-07" passage="Governor Cook shall appear at the hearing and be examined by the Committee, its representatives, and her legal counsel, if any." /><Cite s="wh-memo-2026-10-07" passage="The hearing shall be closed to the public but shall be transcribed." /><Cite s="wh-memo-2026-10-07" passage="shall recommend whether there is cause to remove Governor Cook from her position on the Board of Governors of the Federal Reserve" /><Cite s="usc-12-242" passage="unless sooner removed for cause by the President" /></p>
        <p data-layer="narrative">Firing Cook would let Trump nominate a fourth governor to the Fed's seven-member board, whose governors vote on interest rates with five presidents of regional Fed banks. Three of the seven, including the chair, Kevin Warsh, are already his appointees. His nominee would need Senate confirmation.<Cite s="ap-cook-committee-2026-10-09" passage="As a member of the board of governors, Cook is also one of the 12 voting members of the committee." /><Cite s="reuters-fed-seats-2026-08-26" passage="Besides Warsh, Trump would have only two of his own appointees to choose from: Michelle Bowman, who is already the Fed’s vice chair for supervision, and Fed Governor Christopher Waller" /><Cite s="fed-warsh-oath-2026-05-22" passage="President Donald J. Trump nominated Mr. Warsh on March 4, 2026." /><Cite s="fed-board-members" passage="The seven members of the Board of Governors of the Federal Reserve System are nominated by the President and confirmed by the Senate." /></p>
        <p data-layer="narrative" data-answer="3">Trump tried to fire Cook last year. In June the Supreme Court blocked that, because she had not been given a chance to answer the charges, and said he could try again.<Cite s="scotus-trump-v-cook-2026-06-29" passage="the President failed to afford Cook the procedural protections to which she was entitled by statute" /><Cite s="scotus-trump-v-cook-2026-06-29" passage="but not to forbid the President from trying again, if he chooses to do so" /></p>
        <p data-layer="narrative" data-answer="2">Cook denies wrongdoing and has not been charged with a crime. She remains a governor, and her lawyers say they are weighing whether the hearing is genuine before deciding whether she will attend. After the hearing the decision is Trump's, with no deadline.<Cite s="ap-cook-committee-2026-10-09" passage="Cook has denied any wrongdoing and has not been charged with a crime." /><Cite s="ap-cook-committee-2026-10-09" passage="said they were considering having Cook appear at the Nov. 5 hearing" /><Cite s="lowell-eisen-statement-2026-10-09" passage="We have engaged with the White House to evaluate whether the process proposed has a possibility of being a genuine one and not simply a box checking exercise." /><Cite s="cnbc-explainer-2026-10-09" passage="There is no set deadline for that final decision." /></p>
      </section>

      <section id="the-mortgages">
        <h2 data-layer="fact">Who Cook is, and the charge against her</h2>
        <p data-layer="narrative">Cook was a professor of economics at Michigan State University before President Biden appointed her to the Fed's board, which she joined in May 2022 as its first Black woman. He reappointed her in 2023 to a term that runs to January 2038.<Cite s="fed-cook-bio" passage="Dr. Cook was a professor of economics and international relations at Michigan State University" /><Cite s="fed-cook-bio" passage="took office as a member of the Board of Governors of the Federal Reserve System on May 23, 2022" /><Cite s="ap-cook-committee-2026-10-09" passage="Cook was appointed by former President Joe Biden and is the first Black woman on the Fed’s board." /><Cite s="fed-cook-bio" passage="for a term ending January 31, 2038" /></p>
        <p data-layer="narrative">The allegation goes back to 2021, before she joined the Fed. Within two weeks that summer she took out mortgages on a house in Ann Arbor, Michigan, and a condo in Atlanta. The White House says she promised on both that the home would be her main residence that year. That promise can bring a lower rate than a loan on a second home, by 0.25 to 0.50 percentage points by the White House's figure. The White House also says the Atlanta condo was listed for rent in September 2022.<Cite s="wh-scavino-letter-2026-08-05" passage="Just two weeks later, you signed another document for a property in Georgia stating that it would be your primary residence for that same year." /><Cite s="wh-scavino-letter-2026-08-05" passage="interest rates on secondary residence mortgages are typically between 0.25-0.50% higher than their primary residence counterparts" /><Cite s="wh-scavino-letter-2026-08-05" passage="the Atlanta address was then listed for rent in September 2022" /></p>
        <p data-layer="narrative">The charge came from Bill Pulte, director of the Federal Housing Finance Agency, who sent a criminal referral to the Justice Department on August 15, 2025.<Cite s="wh-scavino-letter-2026-08-05" passage="On August 15, 2025, Director of the Federal Housing Finance Agency William J. Pulte submitted a criminal referral to the Department of Justice" /> The next month AP, citing one person familiar with the matter, reported that Justice Department investigators had issued subpoenas in an inquiry into the two homes. The department declined to comment, and no charge has followed.<Cite s="ap-doj-probe-2025-09-04" passage="Investigators have issued subpoenas as part of an inquiry into Cook that was spawned by a criminal referral from the country’s top housing regulator" /><Cite s="ap-doj-probe-2025-09-04" passage="A Justice Department spokesperson declined to comment on the probe" /><Cite s="ap-cook-committee-2026-10-09" passage="has not been charged with a crime" /></p>
        <p data-layer="narrative">Cook denies it. Her lawyers say she signed the Atlanta mortgage as her primary residence by "an entirely inadvertent oversight," and that it was not fraud.<Cite s="lowell-response-2026-08-26" passage="Governor Cook’s signing of a “primary” mortgage agreement" /><Cite s="lowell-response-2026-08-26" passage="was an entirely inadvertent oversight" /><Cite s="lowell-response-2026-08-26" passage="An inadvertent oversight is not fraudulent or criminal." /> They say she briefly listed the Atlanta condo for rent but decided not to rent it.<Cite s="lowell-response-2026-08-26" passage="though Governor Cook briefly listed the property for rent, she ultimately decided not to rent it" /></p>
      </section>

      <section id="why-now">
        <h2 data-layer="fact">Why a hearing now</h2>
        <p data-layer="narrative">On August 20, 2025, Pulte posted pages of his referral, and within thirty minutes Trump posted "Cook must resign, now!!!" Five days later he sent her a letter firing her. She sued, and a federal judge, Jia Cobb, kept her in her job.<Cite s="dcd-cook-opinion-2025-09-09" passage="Within thirty minutes, President Trump posted on the social media platform Truth Social that “Cook must resign, now!!!,”" /><Cite s="scotus-trump-v-cook-2026-06-29" passage="the President’s letter of August 25" /><Cite s="scotus-trump-v-cook-2026-06-29" passage="After Cook filed suit, the District Court issued a preliminary injunction to prevent her removal." /></p>
        <p data-layer="narrative">The law gives governors 14-year terms and does not say what cause is.<Cite s="usc-12-242" passage="each member shall hold office for a term of fourteen years from the expiration of the term of his predecessor, unless sooner removed for cause by the President" /> On June 29 the Supreme Court, 5 to 4, kept Cook in office. It held that she was entitled to "some explanation of the evidence at issue, some avenue for a response, and a deadline by which a response would be due," and had been given none. It did not decide whether she had done anything wrong, and it said Trump could try again.<Cite s="scotus-trump-v-cook-2026-06-29" passage="At minimum, Cook was entitled to some explanation of the evidence at issue, some avenue for a response, and a deadline by which a response would be due." /><Cite s="scotus-trump-v-cook-2026-06-29" passage="In this opinion, we have not addressed the facts" /><Cite s="scotus-trump-v-cook-2026-06-29" passage="but not to forbid the President from trying again, if he chooses to do so" /> The Court said that chance to respond could be "on written materials only," with no hearing in person.<Cite s="scotus-trump-v-cook-2026-06-29" passage="We see no reason why that opportunity “may not be had on written materials only,” with no oral presentation." /></p>
        <p data-layer="narrative">Trump tried again in writing first. On August 5 the White House sent Cook notice that he was considering removing her, with the evidence and 21 days to answer, and her lawyers answered on August 26.<Cite s="wh-scavino-letter-2026-08-05" passage="you are hereby provided notice that the President is considering removing you from your position on the Board of Governors of the Federal Reserve" /><Cite s="wh-scavino-letter-2026-08-05" passage="Please provide a written response with your explanation for your false statements and accompanying evidence within 21 days." /><Cite s="lowell-response-2026-08-26" passage="Response by Governor Lisa D. Cook to August 5, 2026, Notice of Potential Removal" /> On October 7 he signed the memo that adds the committee and the hearing in person. The memo does not mention her lawyers' answer, and gives no reason why the written exchange was not enough.<Cite s="fox-cook-2026-10-09" passage="according to a notice dated Wednesday, Oct. 7, 2026, but released on Friday by the Trump administration" /><Cite s="wh-memo-2026-10-07" passage="the Committee shall investigate the allegations against Governor Cook" /></p>
        <p data-layer="narrative">The committee's members are Kevin Hassett, Trump's chief economic adviser; Andrea Lucas, who chairs the Equal Employment Opportunity Commission; and Keith Sonderling, the labor secretary and acting head of the Office of Government Ethics.<Cite s="wh-memo-2026-10-07" passage="The Assistant to the President for Economic Policy, the Chairman of the Equal Employment Opportunity Commission, and the Director of the Office of Government Ethics shall serve as members of the Committee." /><Cite s="reuters-cook-inquiry-2026-10-09" passage="Trump's chief economic policy advisor Kevin Hassett, Equal Employment Opportunity Commission chair Andrea Lucas, and acting Office of Government Ethics director Keith Sonderling will comprise the newly formed committee" /><Cite s="wsj-cook-hearing-2026-10-09" passage="the ethics office’s current acting director, Keith Sonderling, who also serves as labor secretary" /> The hearing may last up to four hours. The committee, or Justice Department lawyers it chooses, will question Cook, and she may bring a lawyer, argument, written evidence and written statements from witnesses.<Cite s="wh-memo-2026-10-07" passage="The hearing shall occur at the White House and shall last no longer than 4 hours." /><Cite s="wh-memo-2026-10-07" passage="it may authorize its representatives, including Department of Justice personnel, to conduct that questioning" /><Cite s="wh-memo-2026-10-07" passage="Governor Cook may present argument, written evidence, and written witness statements regarding the evidence and allegations." /></p>
        <SourcedBlock source="scotus-trump-v-cook-2026-06-29" kind="record" detail>
          <figure class="story-figure">
            <figcaption><strong>From the mortgages to the hearing</strong></figcaption>
            <DataTable columns={["Date", "What happened"]} rows={chronology.rows} ariaLabel="Dates from Cook's 2021 mortgages to the November 5 hearing" />
          </figure>
          <p>Cook sued on August 28, 2025, and Judge Jia Cobb kept her in office on September 9. The Supreme Court heard the case on January 21.<Cite s="dcd-cook-opinion-2025-09-09" passage="Cook filed a six-count complaint on August 28, 2025." /><Cite s="scotus-oral-argument-2026-01-21" passage="Deceit or gross negligence by a" /></p>
        </SourcedBlock>
      </section>

      <section id="the-sides">
        <h2 data-layer="fact">Is a mortgage form reason to fire her?</h2>
        <p data-layer="narrative">Neither side disputes that she signed both forms, and the Supreme Court has ruled that she must have a chance to answer before any firing. The question is whether what she signed is cause.<Cite s="lowell-response-2026-08-26" passage="Governor Cook’s signing of a “primary” mortgage agreement" /><Cite s="scotus-trump-v-cook-2026-06-29" passage="Only after Cook has had the opportunity to respond to the charges made against her" /></p>
        <h3 data-layer="fact">Why Trump says it is</h3>
        <p data-layer="narrative">Trump's memo says it is his job to fire "subordinates who cannot be trusted to tell the truth and follow the law."<Cite s="wh-memo-2026-10-07" passage="As President, it is my job to ensure the laws are faithfully executed, including by firing subordinates who cannot be trusted to tell the truth and follow the law." /> His lawyers told the Supreme Court that deceit or gross negligence by a financial regulator in her own financial dealings is cause. The White House's August letter said it was "impossible that you intended to honor both" promises.<Cite s="scotus-oral-argument-2026-01-21" passage="Deceit or gross negligence by a" /><Cite s="scotus-oral-argument-2026-01-21" passage="financial regulator in financial transactions" /><Cite s="wh-scavino-letter-2026-08-05" passage="The President believes it is inconceivable that you were not aware of your first commitment when making the second and impossible that you intended to honor both." /></p>
        <h3 data-layer="fact">Why Cook says it is not</h3>
        <p data-layer="narrative">Cook's lawyers say the Atlanta form was an honest mistake, made in her private life before she joined the Fed, and that neither the White House nor Pulte says she actually got a lower rate. They say she began by using the lender's website to ask about a "vacation home" mortgage.<Cite s="lowell-response-2026-08-26" passage="The pre-office conduct at issue, all of which took place in Governor Cook’s private capacity" /><Cite s="lowell-response-2026-08-26" passage="neither the Letter nor Director Pulte’s Referral declares that Governor Cook actually received a preferential interest rate on any property" /><Cite s="lowell-response-2026-08-26" passage="began with Governor Cook using the lender’s website to seek information about a “vacation home” mortgage" /></p>
        <p data-layer="narrative">A printout of rate-search results from the lender's website, dated May 28, 2021, lists the Atlanta condo's use as "Vacation Home," and her lawyers say it went to the same lender. It is a rate quote for a 15-year mortgage, and it says the real terms come only in an official loan estimate. The Atlanta mortgage she signed in July was for 30 years.<Cite s="lowell-response-2025-11-17" passage="Bank-Fund Staff Federal Credit Union - Rate Search Results" /><Cite s="lowell-response-2025-11-17" passage="Property Use: Vacation Home" /><Cite s="lowell-response-2025-11-17" passage="A separate, contemporaneous May 28, 2021 loan document provided to the same lender clearly states the Atlanta condo’s “Property Use” as a “Vacation Home.”" /><Cite s="lowell-response-2025-11-17" passage="Conforming 15 Yr Fixed" /><Cite s="lowell-response-2025-11-17" passage="Your actual rate, payment, and costs could be higher. Get an official Loan Estimate before choosing a loan." /><Cite s="reuters-cook-mortgages-factbox-2025-08-26" passage="Again the address is not listed but the value and term of the loan matches her $540,000 mortgage on her Atlanta, Georgia condominium." /> On a form for a security clearance she called the condo a "second home," and she has never claimed Georgia's homestead exemption on it, a property-tax break for a primary residence.<Cite s="ap-vacation-home-2025-09-12" passage="Documents obtained by The Associated Press also showed that on a second form submitted by Cook to gain a security clearance, she described the property as a “second home.”" /><Cite s="ap-vacation-home-2025-09-12" passage="Fulton County tax records show Cook has never claimed a homestead exemption on the condo, which allows someone who uses a property as their primary residence to reduce their property taxes" /></p>
        <p data-layer="narrative">Her lawyers also call the case selective. They cite reports that Trump took out primary-residence mortgages on two Florida homes seven weeks apart in 1993, and that Treasury Secretary Scott Bessent and Attorney General Todd Blanche did the same on more than one home. Pulte has made no public referral against any of them.<Cite s="lowell-response-2026-08-26" passage="In 1993, President Trump reportedly acquired “primary” residence mortgages for two different Florida homes just seven weeks apart." /><Cite s="lowell-response-2026-08-26" passage="Treasury Secretary Scott Bessent and Attorney General Todd Blanche have both entered into “primary” residence mortgages for multiple homes." /><Cite s="lowell-response-2026-08-26" passage="Director Pulte has made no public referrals against President Trump himself nor any of his cabinet members referred to in this response" /></p>
        <h3 data-layer="fact">What the courts have said</h3>
        <p data-layer="narrative">Judge Cobb held last year that conduct from before a governor took office cannot, by itself, be cause. The Supreme Court did not adopt her view or the government's, and left the question open.<Cite s="dcd-cook-opinion-2025-09-09" passage="“For cause” thus does not contemplate removing an individual purely for conduct that occurred before they began in office." /><Cite s="scotus-trump-v-cook-2026-06-29" passage="The Court rejects both parties’ positions." /> It said cause is "a substantial threshold," a high bar, judged by how serious the misconduct is and how it bears on the job. A court may also ask whether a firing is really a way to put in someone more agreeable.<Cite s="scotus-trump-v-cook-2026-06-29" passage="That counsels a substantial threshold for “cause.”" /><Cite s="scotus-trump-v-cook-2026-06-29" passage="will depend, at least in part, on the seriousness of the alleged misconduct, and the extent of any nexus that may exist to the Governor’s professional duties" /><Cite s="scotus-trump-v-cook-2026-06-29" passage="or whether it simply represents an effort to secure a “more congenial” replacement" /></p>
        <h3 data-layer="fact">Is this about interest rates?</h3>
        <p data-layer="narrative">The government told the Supreme Court that "for cause" rules out removing a governor "for policy disagreement," but covers concerns about her integrity, the reason Trump gives.<Cite s="scotus-trump-v-cook-2026-06-29" passage="acknowledging that the “ ‘for cause’ provision rules out removal for no reason at all, or for policy disagreement”" /><Cite s="scotus-trump-v-cook-2026-06-29" passage="That excludes a “mere policy disagreement,” according to the Government" /> On the evening of October 10 Trump said he formed the committee "because I think she's dishonest," and added: "And she’s an automatic no vote, and that's not a good thing."<Cite s="doyle-trump-remarks-2026-10-10" passage="tonight that he formed a committee to investigate the allegations against Fed Governor Lisa Cook" /><Cite s="doyle-trump-remarks-2026-10-10" passage="because I think she's dishonest" /><Cite s="doyle-trump-remarks-2026-10-10" passage="And she’s an automatic no vote, and that's not a good thing." /></p>
        <p data-layer="narrative">Cook has voted with the majority on every rate decision. On September 16 the Fed raised rates 12 to 0, with Cook voting alongside Trump's three appointees, Warsh, Michelle Bowman and Christopher Waller. Trump answered that rates "should be 1%, or less."<Cite s="reuters-cook-inquiry-2026-10-09" passage="She has always cast her vote with the majority on the Fed's policy-making committee" /><Cite s="fomc-minutes-2026-09-16" passage="Voting for this action: Kevin Warsh, John C. Williams, Michael S. Barr, Michelle W. Bowman, Lisa D. Cook, Beth M. Hammack, Philip N. Jefferson, Neel Kashkari, Lorie K. Logan, Anna Paulson, Jerome H. Powell, and Christopher J. Waller." /><Cite s="the-hill-trump-fed-reaction-2026-09-16" passage="Interest Rates in the United States should be 1%, or less" /></p>
        <h3 data-layer="fact">The hearing itself</h3>
        <p data-layer="narrative">Cook's lawyers say Trump's "repeated statements that he has already decided to fire" her give them grave doubts about the hearing. They also say she welcomes the chance to "present the facts so she can clear her name."<Cite s="lowell-eisen-statement-2026-10-09" passage="In light of the President’s repeated statements that he has already decided to fire Governor Cook" /><Cite s="lowell-eisen-statement-2026-10-09" passage="Governor Cook welcomes the opportunity to present the facts so she can clear her name and demonstrate there is no legal basis to fire her." /> Their particular objection is Hassett. On August 27, 2025, two days after Trump's letter firing her, Hassett said she should take leave from the Fed during the legal fight. Her lawyer Abbe Lowell has asked the White House to explain why Hassett is on the committee, and what role the Justice Department will play.<Cite s="wtimes-hassett-2025-08-27" passage="so she should take leave from the Fed during the looming legal fight" /><Cite s="cnn-lowell-2026-10-09" passage="What they need to do is explain why did they put Mr. Hassett on this committee." /><Cite s="cnn-lowell-2026-10-09" passage="I don't know what role that would be and why." /></p>
      </section>

      <section id="the-catch">
        <aside class="the-catch" data-layer="fact">
          <p class="tt-label">The catch</p>
          <ol>
            <li>
              <h3>What the Supreme Court decided</h3>
              <p data-layer="narrative">The Guardian wrote that the Supreme Court found her earlier firing "unconstitutional," and Mediaite that the Court said Trump "did not have the power to fire a member of the Federal Reserve." Both overstate the ruling. The Court found only that the law entitled Cook to notice and a chance to respond. It set aside her constitutional argument and said Trump could try again.<Cite s="guardian-cook-2026-10-09" passage="despite a June supreme court ruling that her earlier firing was unconstitutional" /><Cite s="mediaite-cook-2026-10-09" passage="saying the commander in chief did not have the power to fire a member of the Federal Reserve" /><Cite s="scotus-trump-v-cook-2026-06-29" passage="The Court need not address Cook’s constitutional due process argument" /><Cite s="scotus-trump-v-cook-2026-06-29" passage="but not to forbid the President from trying again, if he chooses to do so" /></p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <h3>"A transcript would be published"</h3>
              <p data-layer="narrative">AP's report, carried by PBS and others, said "a transcript would be published, the memo said," and the Guardian wrote that the hearing would be "transcribed and published." The memo says the hearing "shall be transcribed." It does not say the transcript will be released.<Cite s="ap-cook-committee-2026-10-09" passage="The hearing wouldn’t be public but a transcript would be published, the memo said." /><Cite s="guardian-cook-2026-10-09" passage="which will be closed to the public but transcribed and published" /><Cite s="wh-memo-2026-10-07" passage="The hearing shall be closed to the public but shall be transcribed." /></p>
            </li>
          </ol>
        </aside>
      </section>

      <section id="next">
        <h2 data-layer="fact">What happens next</h2>
        <p data-layer="narrative" data-answer="5">Cook may send the committee a written statement, which must arrive by {computed.statementDue}, three days before the hearing. After the hearing on November 5, both sides in her lawsuit over the first firing owe Judge Cobb a joint report on the case the next day. Cook may send a second written statement by November 10.<Cite s="wh-memo-2026-10-07" passage="provided the statement is received at least 3 days before the hearing" /><Cite s="cobb-minute-order-2026-09-22" passage="The Parties are ORDERED to confer and submit a joint status report by November 6, 2026." /><Cite s="wh-memo-2026-10-07" passage="Governor Cook may submit a post-hearing written statement to the Committee by November 10, 2026." /></p>
        <p data-layer="narrative">The committee then reports to Trump "as soon as practicable," and no deadline binds his decision. If he fires her, she can go back to court, and only then will judges weigh whether the charges amount to cause.<Cite s="wh-memo-2026-10-07" passage="As soon as practicable after receipt of that statement" /><Cite s="cnbc-explainer-2026-10-09" passage="There is no set deadline for that final decision." /><Cite s="scotus-trump-v-cook-2026-06-29" passage="At that point, Cook may seek judicial review under the framework described in this opinion" /><Cite s="scotus-trump-v-cook-2026-06-29" passage="And only then can the courts assess the validity and sufficiency of such charges." /></p>
        <p data-layer="narrative">Until Trump decides, Cook is still a governor, with a vote at the Fed's next rate meeting on October 28.<Cite s="cnbc-explainer-2026-10-09" passage="upcoming vote on interest rates scheduled for Oct. 28" /></p>
        <SourcedBlock source="cnbc-explainer-2026-10-09" kind="checked" detail>
          <p>The memo does not say whether Cook's answers at the hearing could be used against her in a criminal case.<Cite s="cnbc-explainer-2026-10-09" passage="but it does not say whether her answers would be protected from use in a later criminal case" /></p>
        </SourcedBlock>
      </section>

      <section id="records">
      <StorySources event={event.slug} />
      <StoryState view={state} supporting />
      </section>
    </main>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>}
